    faq: [{ question: "What standard does this use?", answer: "WCAG 2.1 relative luminance contrast ratio with AA and AAA badges for normal and large text entirely offline. Dark #1a1a1a on #ffffff scores 12.6 to 1 and passes AAA, while medium gray #767676 on white reaches 4.54 to 1 and passes AA. Light #999999 stalls near 2.85 to 1 and fails. All luminance arithmetic remains local in your tab." }, { question: "Does it check large text too?", answer: "Yes. Normal text needs 4.5 to 1 for AA and 7 to 1 for AAA, while large text above 18pt or 14pt bold needs 3 to 1 for AA and 4.5 to 1 for AAA. Navy #1e3a5f on mist #f8fafc passes both tiers comfortably. Orange #ff5733 on white fails body copy yet may pass oversized headlines, so test each size independently." }, { question: "What ratio do I need to pass WCAG AA?", answer: "Aim at least 4.5 to 1 for paragraphs and 3 to 1 for large headings to satisfy AA, the legal baseline in most policies. AAA at 7 to 1 suits government portals and low-vision audiences. For example darken #999999 to #595959 to jump from fail to AA on white backgrounds without altering layout." }, { question: "Why does my brand orange fail on white?", answer: "Saturated hues like coral #ff5733 carry high luminance that compresses distance against white #ffffff to roughly 3 to 1, below body thresholds. Keep orange for large headlines or icons, and pair it with deep charcoal #1a1a1a buttons for readable actions. Verify replacement #c2410c burnt orange which climbs near 4.6 to 1 while preserving warmth in our tool at /color-converter." }, { question: "How do I fix a failing button without redesigning?", answer: "Darken the background or lighten the label incrementally until badges flip green, preserving hue identity. Shift gray #999999 to #595959, or deepen teal #14b8a6 to #0f766e for white labels. Retest hover states separately because darkening on hover can dip below thresholds. Export approved pairs as tokens for stylesheets and Figma libraries." }],
